#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166941 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "113b981d-bbf2-588a-be74-028506cdb19d" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Phase Portrait of a System"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166942 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b542bd66-f4f8-5b4e-a3e7-4717827c427a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Parameters of a System Given Its Phase Portrait"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166943 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f0b176c-c802-5b3b-85d0-dede602c9bf3" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Systems of Linear Differential Equations With Complex Eigenvalues"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168009 :tutorial/content "In this topic, we'll study phase portraits of linear systems\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t),\n\\qquad \n\\mathbf{x}(t)={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( t ) \\\\ y ( t )\\end{array} \\right]},\n$$\nwhere the eigenvalues of a $2\\times2$ matrix $A$ are\n$$\n\\lambda_{1,2}=a\\pm b\\text{i},\n\\qquad b\\neq 0.\n$$\n\nLet $\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i},$ where $\\mathbf{p},\\mathbf{q} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = a + b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) = \\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {\\mathbf{x}}_{\\text{complex}} ( t ) & \\displaystyle  = \\mathbf{v} {e}^{( a + b \\text{i} ) t} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {e}^{a t} ( \\cos   ( b t ) + \\text{i} \\sin   ( b t ) ) ( \\mathbf{p} + \\mathbf{q} \\text{i} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {e}^{a t} ( \\cos   ( b t ) \\mathbf{p} - \\sin   ( b t ) \\mathbf{q} ) + \\text{i} {e}^{a t} ( \\sin   ( b t ) \\mathbf{p} + \\cos   ( b t ) \\mathbf{q} ) .\\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \nc_1 e^{at}\\big(\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big) + \nc_2 e^{at}\\big(\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big).\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\cos(bt)$ and $\\sin(bt)$ are bounded, so the factor $e^{at}$ controls the long-term behavior:\n\n- If $a>0,$ then $e^{at}\\to\\infty$ as $t\\to\\infty.$ So, trajectories are *unbounded*, and we have a *spiral* that is traversed toward infinity as $t\\to\\infty.$ In particular, the trajectory is unbounded for $t \\geq 0.$\n\n- If $a<0,$ then $e^{at}\\to 0$ as $t\\to\\infty.$ So, trajectories *approach the origin*, and we have a *spiral* that is traversed toward the origin as $t\\to\\infty.$ In particular, the trajectory is bounded for $t \\ge  0 ,$ although it is unbounded if $t$ is allowed to approach $-\\infty.$\n\n- If $a=0,$ then $e^{at}=1$ for all $t.$ So, trajectories are *bounded*, and we have an *ellipse*.\n\nLet's see some concrete examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "2a925aab-4f30-57a3-a3a2-6c3e21b5b318" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14482 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168010 :tutorial/content "Consider the system of linear differential equations below.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}x(t)-4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4x(t)+\\frac{1}{2}y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_{1,2} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\pm4\\text{i},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_{1,2} = [\\pm \\text{i}, 1]^T,$ respectively. Given that the general solution of the system is\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( t ) \\\\ y ( t )\\end{array} \\right]} = \nc_1 e^{t/2} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}\\cos   ( 4 t ) \\\\ \\sin   ( 4 t )\\end{array} \\right]} + \nc_2 e^{t/2} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- \\sin   ( 4 t ) \\\\ \\cos   ( 4 t )\\end{array} \\right]},\n\\qquad c_1,c_2 \\in \\mathbb{R},\n$$\nwe are interested in sketching the phase portrait of the system of differential equations given above.\n\nIf we set $c_1 = 1$ and $c_2 = 0$ in the general solution of the system, we obtain the particular solution:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x={e}^{t/2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ y={e}^{t/2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$t$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{\\pi}{16}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.78$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.78$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.22$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLet's sketch this solution in the phase space.\n\n![](images/4f/4f9da4b66977b98b983477d22abb6e00135ea0e1bfa9f785cd0a62d674a7b782.png)\n\nNotice that $|\\sin(4t)| \\leq 1$ and $|\\cos(4t)| \\leq 1,$ but $e^{t/2} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty.$ Thus, the obtained curve is unbounded. So, we have a spiral that is traversed counterclockwise.\n\nAgain, since $e^{t/2} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ the spiral is traversed toward infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/62/62380812d6fbe4e28531a455814296a4969a0744fb28a94a5e2d7c588ec5cfc4.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "f3d4213d-a0e4-5bda-9e29-8eca4801de63" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14483 :tutorial/title "Systems With Complex Eigenvalues"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168025 :tutorial/content "Finally, let’s summarize what we’ve learned so far.\n\nAgain, we are considering a linear system\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t),\n\\qquad \n\\mathbf{x}(t)={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( t ) \\\\ y ( t )\\end{array} \\right]},\n$$\nwhere the eigenvalues of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_{1,2}=a\\pm bi,\n\\qquad b\\neq 0.\n$$\n\nAs for the classification of the equilibrium at the origin, we get the following:\n\n- The type of the curve is determined by the parameter $a{:}$\n\n- If $a=0,$ we have bounded closed curves (ellipses/circles).\n\n- If $a \\neq 0,$ we have spirals.\n\n- The stability is determined by the sign of $a{:}$\n\n- If $a>0,$ we have an **unstable spiral** (source).\n\n- If $a<0,$ we have a **stable spiral** (sink).\n\n- If $a=0,$ we have a **center**.\n\n**Note:** The center is a *stable* equilibrium but not *asymptotically stable* since trajectories do not converge to it.\n\nLet's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "c36d256f-edd3-5079-9f72-da6e3a8b4f91" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14575 :tutorial/title "Classification of Equilibria"}]}
